1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,490
This is an LLM gateway, built and run the way an SRE team would run it on Google Kubernetes Engine.

2
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:22,871
A Go service sits in front of Gemini on Vertex AI and gives every team an OpenAI-compatible endpoint with its own API key, request and token quotas, an exact and a semantic cache in Memorystore, and automatic fallback to a cheaper model behind a circuit breaker.

3
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:31,874
It runs on a GKE Standard cluster with an on-demand system pool and a Spot pool for the gateway, behind a global load balancer with Cloud Armor.

4
00:00:31,874 --> 00:00:35,187
Managed Prometheus feeds two SLOs and the autoscaler.

5
00:00:35,187 --> 00:00:39,564
Every resource is Terraform, and nothing in the path holds a key file.

6
00:00:40,777 --> 00:00:43,341
First, a real request through the load balancer.

7
00:00:43,341 --> 00:00:49,110
The models endpoint lists what this tenant may use, with the fallback and the list price per million tokens.

8
00:00:49,110 --> 00:01:00,541
Then a streamed chat completion with the cache bypassed, so this one goes to the model: the gateway translates it to Gemini's streaming API, relays the tokens as server-sent events, and reports time to first token.

9
00:01:00,541 --> 00:01:05,401
That first-token time is the latency a user feels, so it is the metric the SLO is built on.

10
00:01:06,796 --> 00:01:14,589
The same question again is an exact cache hit: the answer comes back from Redis in a few milliseconds, with the x-cache header set to exact.

11
00:01:14,589 --> 00:01:25,776
A paraphrase of the question is a semantic hit: the gateway embeds the prompt, finds a stored prompt with cosine similarity above zero point nine five, and returns its answer without calling the model.

12
00:01:25,776 --> 00:01:31,732
Both caches are namespaced per tenant and per model, so one team can never be served another team's answer.

13
00:01:33,130 --> 00:01:35,464
Tenants are enforced at the gateway.

14
00:01:35,464 --> 00:01:42,077
team-support may only use flash-lite, so asking for the bigger model is a 403, not a silent downgrade.

15
00:01:42,077 --> 00:01:51,802
team-burst has a bucket of ten requests and a refill of thirty per minute: the first ten pass, then the gateway returns 429 with a Retry-After header.

16
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:56,794
The bucket lives in Redis as a Lua script, so every replica shares one count.

17
00:01:56,794 --> 00:02:01,786
There is also a daily token budget per tenant, exposed in the budget headers.

18
00:02:03,213 --> 00:02:22,218
The dashboard is twelve PromQL panels in Cloud Monitoring: first-token latency at p50 and p95, requests per second by outcome, tokens per second by model, cache hit ratio, estimated cost per hour per tenant, rate-limit rejections, upstream status codes, breaker state, and in-flight requests against replicas.

19
00:02:22,218 --> 00:02:26,277
Cost attribution per tenant comes for free from the same counters.

20
00:02:28,739 --> 00:02:38,243
Meanwhile a k6 load test has been running inside the cluster: a warm-cache scenario, a ramp of unique streaming prompts, and a low-quota tenant bursting.

21
00:02:38,243 --> 00:02:49,610
The autoscaler does not watch CPU, which stays low while pods wait on the model; it scales on in-flight requests per pod, a Prometheus gauge served through the custom metrics adapter.

22
00:02:49,610 --> 00:02:55,883
The deployment has grown from two replicas, and the cluster autoscaler added Spot nodes to hold them.

23
00:02:57,253 --> 00:02:58,327
Now a fault drill.

24
00:02:58,327 --> 00:03:04,230
Through the admin endpoint I tell every replica to treat the primary model as failing for a minute.

25
00:03:04,230 --> 00:03:18,303
Requests keep succeeding, but the x-model-served header shows flash-lite: after a few failures the circuit breaker for the primary opens and traffic skips it entirely, then a single probe request closes it again when the minute is over.

26
00:03:18,303 --> 00:03:22,477
The breaker gauge and the fallback counter are visible in the metrics.

27
00:03:29,452 --> 00:03:34,237
Cloud Armor sits at the edge, attached to the backend through a Gateway policy.

28
00:03:34,237 --> 00:03:48,350
A request carrying an SQL injection payload is rejected with 403 by the preconfigured WAF rule before it reaches a pod, and a burst of two hundred requests from one address is throttled to one hundred and twenty per minute with 429s.

29
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:52,348
Tenant quotas protect the model budget; this protects the cluster.

30
00:03:53,735 --> 00:04:07,767
Two SLOs are defined in Terraform on the Prometheus metrics: ninety-nine point five percent of chat completions without a gateway or provider error, and ninety-five percent of streams delivering their first token within three seconds.

31
00:04:07,767 --> 00:04:19,041
That number came from the load test: cold first-token p95 was two point zero four seconds, so the objective was set where the system is, and two seconds is the stretch goal the cache buys.

32
00:04:19,041 --> 00:04:22,578
Quota rejections count as good; they are correct behaviour.

33
00:04:22,578 --> 00:04:28,875
Each SLO has a multi-window burn-rate alert, fourteen point four x over one hour or six x over six hours.

34
00:04:28,875 --> 00:04:34,991
The k6 thresholds encode the same targets, so a regression fails the load test before it burns budget.

35
00:04:36,378 --> 00:05:04,386
The security posture, verified: private nodes with Cloud NAT and a control plane limited to one network range; Workload Identity from the Kubernetes service account to a Google service account with exactly the roles it needs; API keys, the admin key and the Redis credentials read from Secret Manager, never from a Kubernetes secret; a default-deny network policy; and pods that are non-root, read-only and capability-free on a Pod Security restricted namespace.

36
00:05:05,775 --> 00:05:19,279
Everything shown is in the repository: the Go gateway with its tests, Terraform for the cluster, Memorystore, Cloud Armor, secrets, identities, SLOs and dashboard, the kustomize manifests, the k6 profile and a capacity and cost model.

37
00:05:19,279 --> 00:05:25,165
The whole platform costs a few hundred rupees a day while it runs, and the teardown script removes it.

38
00:05:25,165 --> 00:05:26,319
Thanks for watching.
